Navigation system with Sobel filter
===================================

A navigation system split over three partitions that share data, to study CPU,
memory and I/O interference between communicating partitions.

What it tests
-------------

Inter-partition communication through sampling ports next to a CPU- and memory-
heavy partition.

What it does
------------

Three partitions: GPS_P, PROC_P and SOBE_P.

GPS_P generates GPS positions (latitude, longitude) and writes them to a
sampling port. PROC_P reads them and computes speed and bearing (SPDI) and
altitude and pressure (ALPR). SOBE_P runs a Sobel edge filter on a 200x200
image. Processes inside a partition share data under a semaphore. Windows: 2.5
s, 7.5 s and 5 s.

Expected output
---------------

"NEW POINT GENERATED", "Distance ... Speed ... Bearing", "Altitude ...
Pressure" (some output only with the VERBOSE_* options).

Build and run
-------------

From the top-level directory (builds every benchmark; QEMU needs only the one):

    make benchmarks                     # x86_64
    make BSP=arm64-qemu benchmarks      # arm64

    qemu-system-x86_64 -vga none -m 1G -nographic \
        -kernel benchmark/NAV_SOB_SYS/build/x86_64-qemu/pok.elf
    qemu-system-aarch64 -M virt -cpu cortex-a53 -m 1G -nographic \
        -kernel benchmark/NAV_SOB_SYS/build/arm64-qemu/pok.elf

To build only this benchmark, run "scons bsp=x86_64-qemu" (or bsp=arm64-qemu)
in this directory with POK_PATH set. The benchmark runs forever; quit QEMU with
Ctrl-A X.
